<main class="guide legal wrap" id="main">
  <header class="docs-header">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      What this site and our app in ChatGPT receive, what they keep, and who else takes part.
    </p>
    <p class="legal-date">Updated {{ site.legalUpdated }}</p>
  </header>

  <section aria-labelledby="short">
    <h2 id="short">In short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No account, no cookies, no analytics and no advertising.</li>
      <li>An activity is built and played in your browser. A file you add stays on your device and in ChatGPT: it never reaches a server of ours.</li>
      <li>Our app's server checks the text of the activity the chat writes, and keeps nothing of it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="who">
    <h2 id="who">Who we are</h2>
    <p>
      {{ site.name }} makes free, open-source tools that run in the browser. We run this site,
      {{ site.url | replace("https://", "") }}, and the app {{ site.app.name }} in ChatGPT
      (“the app”). Write to us at <a href="mailto:{{ site.contact }}">{{ site.contact }}</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="app">
    <h2 id="app">The app in ChatGPT</h2>
    <p>
      When ChatGPT uses the app, ChatGPT's servers send a request to our server,
      {{ site.app.server }}, which runs on Cloudflare. A request carries:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The activity's source:</strong> its title and text, its questions and answers, as
        the chat wrote them from your conversation. Anything you put in your request, personal
        data included, can be in it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>For a file you attached, ChatGPT's reference to it:</strong> an id, the file's name
        and type, and an address in ChatGPT's file store. Our server reads the id, the name and the
        type, and hands them to the app's card in your chat. It never opens the address and never
        receives the file.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Details ChatGPT adds to each request,</strong> such as your language, an
        approximate location, and anonymous ids for you and for the conversation. Our server does
        not use them.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Our server checks the source against the rules of its H5P content type and answers whether
      it is valid, with a short outline of what it holds. That is the only use: to show you the
      activity you asked for. In the terms of the EU's GDPR, we process the request because you
      ask for it by using the app (Article 6(1)(b)).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It keeps nothing.</strong> It writes no log of a request, has no database and stores
      nothing. Cloudflare's logs of the server's requests are switched off.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="play">
    <h2 id="play">Playing and saving the activity</h2>
    <p>The app's card in your chat shows this site's play page, which builds the activity and plays it in your browser.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A file you attached is fetched by your browser straight from ChatGPT's file store, and goes nowhere else.</li>
      <li>The chat saves an activity's <code>.h5p</code> up to a size it sets, about 8 MB in ChatGPT in November 2026. For a bigger one whose file plays from the chat, Download opens a tab of this site, which fetches the file again, builds the <code>.h5p</code> and saves it to your computer. Where the file is already in the page, the page says how to open Download in a new tab, which saves it the same way.</li>
      <li>Where an activity names a picture or a video by web address, your browser fetches it from that site, and asks YouTube or Vimeo whether a video is there. A video on YouTube, Vimeo or a similar service plays from that service, which may set its own cookies. A few content types load fonts or tools from other services in the same way.</li>
      <li>Your browser keeps the activity's files in its storage for this site, on your device, so that the player can serve them. Clearing this site's data in your browser removes them. Your answers in the activity are not kept.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="site">
    <h2 id="site">This site</h2>
    <p>
      GitHub Pages hosts this site. GitHub logs the IP address of each visitor for security, as
      the controller of that data. The site sets no cookies and runs no analytics. If you choose a
      light or a dark theme, your browser remembers the choice for this site. The build page works
      as the play page does: what you build stays in your browser.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="others">
    <h2 id="others">Who else takes part</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>OpenAI</strong> runs ChatGPT and decides what it sends to the app. See <a href="https://openai.com/policies/privacy-policy/">OpenAI's privacy policy</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> runs our server, as our processor. For our account it counts the requests, their outcomes and the time they took, without their content, and shows those counts for up to three months. See <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>GitHub</strong> hosts this site. See <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>The sites an activity names.</strong> An activity can show a picture, a video or other content from any web address, YouTube and Vimeo among them. Your browser then contacts that site, which receives your IP address and handles it under its own privacy policy.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We sell nothing and share nothing: we hold nothing to share.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rights">
    <h2 id="rights">Your choices and rights</h2>
    <p>
      The app and this site keep no personal data about you. The one thing we keep is an email
      you write to us, to answer it: you can ask us to show it to you, correct it or delete it,
      and we delete it when you ask. What your browser keeps for this site, you remove by clearing
      the site's data. For what
      ChatGPT, Cloudflare or GitHub hold, ask them. You may also complain to your data protection
      authority. For any question, write to <a href="mailto:{{ site.contact }}">{{ site.contact }}</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="children">
    <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
    <p>The app and this site are for a general audience. They are not meant for children under 13.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="changes">
    <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
    <p>When what we do changes, this page changes with it, and the date at the top says when.</p>
  </section>
</main>

